# Deploy {{.Environment}}

Do the steps in order.

## 1. Set up the server

SSH in to your server and become root (`sudo -i`). Install Docker:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sh
```

Add a GitHub runner: repo → Settings → Actions → Runners → New self-hosted runner → Linux. Run the commands GitHub shows, with these changes:

- Put it in `/opt/actions-runner`.
- Start `config.sh` with `RUNNER_ALLOW_RUNASROOT=1 ./config.sh ...`.
- When it asks for labels, type `{{.Environment}}`. **The workflow only runs on a runner with this label.**
- Do not run `./run.sh`. Install it as a service instead, so it survives a reboot:

```sh
./svc.sh install root
./svc.sh start
```

The server needs a key to read your repo. Make one and print it:

```sh
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N "" -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
```

Add it on GitHub: repo → Settings → Deploy keys → Add. Leave "write access" off. Then clone:

```sh
git clone git@github.com:OWNER/REPO.git /root/{{.Name}}
```

Create `/root/{{.Name}}/deployments/{{.Environment}}/.env` with this, and replace every `CHANGE_ME`:

```sh
ENVIRONMENT={{.Environment}}
ADDRESS=:4000
{{- if .HasPostgres}}
POSTGRES_USER=app
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
POSTGRES_DB={{.Name}}
GOOSE_DBSTRING=postgres://app:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/{{.Name}}?sslmode=disable
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasRedis}}
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
{{- end}}
{{- if .IsGrpc}}
DOMAIN=api.example.com
{{- else}}
TUNNEL_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME
{{- end}}
```
{{- if .HasPostgres}}

Use the same password in `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` and `GOOSE_DBSTRING`.
{{- end}}
{{- if .IsGrpc}}

## 2. Domain

Add an A record for your `DOMAIN` that points to the server IP. If your DNS is on Cloudflare, turn the proxy off (grey cloud). Ports 80 and 443 must be open.

On first start, Caddy gets an HTTPS certificate by itself. Clients connect to `DOMAIN:443`.
{{- else}}

## 2. Domain

Your domain's DNS must be on Cloudflare.

1. Cloudflare → Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels → create a tunnel.
2. Copy the tunnel token into `TUNNEL_TOKEN` in `.env`.
3. Add a public hostname, for example `api.example.com`, with service `http://{{.Name}}:4000`.

Cloudflare serves HTTPS for you. You do not need to open any ports.
{{- end}}

## 3. Deploy

GitHub → Actions → Deploy {{.Environment}} → Run workflow.

The workflow runs on your server. It pulls the code, builds it, {{if .HasPostgres}}runs the database migration, {{end}}and restarts the app.

**It always deploys the `main` branch**, even if you pick another branch in GitHub.

## Add your own environment variables

Add them to `/root/{{.Name}}/deployments/{{.Environment}}/.env` on the server. Then run the Deploy workflow again.

Put secrets (passwords, API keys) there too. Git ignores `.env`, so they stay on the server.
{{- if or .HasPostgres .HasRedis}}

## Reach {{if .HasPostgres}}Postgres{{end}}{{if and .HasPostgres .HasRedis}} and {{end}}{{if .HasRedis}}Redis{{end}}

They only listen on the server itself (`127.0.0.1`), not on the internet. To use them from your computer, open an SSH tunnel and keep it running:

```sh
ssh -N{{if .HasPostgres}} -L 5432:localhost:5432{{end}}{{if .HasRedis}} -L 6379:localhost:6379{{end}} YOUR_USER@SERVER_IP
```

Then, in another terminal, connect to `localhost`:

```sh
{{- if .HasPostgres}}
psql "postgres://app:YOUR_POSTGRES_PASSWORD@localhost:5432/{{.Name}}"
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasRedis}}
redis-cli -h localhost
{{- end}}
```

Port already used on your computer? Pick another local port, for example {{if .HasPostgres}}`-L 15432:localhost:5432`, then connect to port `15432`{{else}}`-L 16379:localhost:6379`, then connect to port `16379`{{end}}.
{{- end}}

## Ports

**Never publish a port as `"5432:5432"` or `"0.0.0.0:5432:5432"` in `docker-compose.yaml`.** Docker skips the firewall (ufw), so that port is open to the internet. Always write `"127.0.0.1:5432:5432"`.

If you put a VPN in front of the server, closing public SSH is up to you.
{{- if .HasPostgres}}

## Database migrations

Each deploy runs `build/migrate.sh` (`goose up`) before it starts the new app. If a migration fails, it is undone, and the old app keeps running.

Rules for every migration:

- Write a working `-- +goose Down`.
- Only add things. Do not drop or rename columns the running app still uses.
- Be careful with `-- +goose NO TRANSACTION` (for example `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`). It can fail halfway. A failed concurrent index stays behind as an invalid index, and you must drop it by hand.
{{- end}}
